with the reveal of the PS 5 Joule sense  controller and all its fancy new bells  and whistles let's take a look back at  the history and evolution of the  PlayStation controller Before we jump  into the nitty-gritty of Sony's flagship  controller it's worth mentioning just  how the Sony Playstation came to be  originally meant to bolster the existing  SNES Sony partnered with Nintendo to  develop a cartridge disk hybrid console  dubbed the Nintendo PlayStation however  after some legal disputes Nintendo  pulled inaudible and partnered with  Philips a Sony competitor leaving Sony's  200 prototype consoles with nowhere to  go luckily for us instead of giving up  on the project  Sony quickly got over the breakup and  eventually released its own console the  PlayStation  [Music]  after circulating around a couple of  different prototypes Sony felt their  controllers resembled to the SNES  controller a little bit too much and  needed to find a way to better  differentiate itself from its former  partner enter the digital controller the  original PlayStation controller  possessed two sets of shoulder buttons  and a shape that had actual handles for  players to hold on to the distinct  design and button layout would  eventually become one of the most  recognizable aspects of the PlayStation  brand with even the triangle square  circle and cross buttons being cemented  as the consoles iconic symbols with  Playstation games making significant  strides in the still new 3d space Sony  would eventually release an enhanced  controller that allowed for more  sophisticated control along with longer  shoulder buttons the dual analog  controller was released with two analog  sticks and a slightly larger shape  compared to its predecessor giving  players a greater range of movement and  precision than ever before  while the introduction of analog sticks  was overall a welcomed addition the use  of it over the traditional d-pad was  still a relatively new idea so much so  that Sony even added an analogue button  in the center of the controller giving  players the option of turning them on  and off the Dual Shock controller  featuring twin analog sticks and haptic  feedback or rumble technology was a  significant upgrade for PlayStation  devices with analogue support on the  rise popular games like Metal Gear Solid  also took advantage of the new rumble  feature by making clever use of the  function during some of the game's most  iconic moments  [Music]  like the old saying goes if it ain't  broke don't fix it  the same could be said for the DualShock  to controller on the outside the  PlayStation 2 s Jill shuck was mostly a  minor upgrade from its ps1 predecessor  with the exception of an old black  finish to accompany its consoles look  beneath the surface however the ps2  controller was lighter and more  functional than that of the original  Jill shock the most noticeable upgrade  was the precious sensitive inputs for  all face and shoulder buttons creating  new ways for gamers to interact with  them games such as Gran Turismo 3 a  speck took advantage of the new feature  as your cars acceleration would greatly  depend on how much pressure you applied  on the buttons having this kind of  real-time feedback expanded the  possibilities of how players could  experience the type of games they love  to play debuting at e3 2005 the  PlayStation 3 was positioned as an  all-in-one home entertainment system  giving consumers access to the then-new  blu-ray disc formats to better utilize  this all-in-one device Sony presented  for the first time a dedicated home  button it was also the first time that  an official PlayStation controller was  completely wireless allowing you to  charge its internal batteries via USB  while these features were undoubtedly  impressive many people were distracted  by its unorthodox design the new design  was intended to reflect the new and more  modern approach of the ps3 but the  overall negative feedback prompted Sony  to go back to the drawing board making  the sixaxis more in line with  controllers that came before it in the  wake of a 2004 patent infringement case  from haptic feedback developer immersion  the ps3 launched - the controller that  lacked Rumble support while the case  made its way through court Sony put more  focus on the devices motion control  capabilities the sixaxis motion control  functionality was a big selling point  for the controller with big games like  Warhawk and lair leaning heavily on the  new movement style however in the years  following its release motion controls  would eventually become a minor feature  for most gamers Sony would eventually  settle the patents infringement case  with immersion and then introduced the  DualShock 3 bringing back the heavily  missed Rumble support  going back to the DualShock name the  ps3s DualShock 3 was a standard 6 access  controller with added vibration feedback  one of the first big games to utilize  the DualShock 3 z' rumble feature was  Metal Gear Solid 4 guns of the Patriots  and much like how the original Metal  Gear Solid broke the fourth wall to  acknowledge the Joe shock mgs4 also  included some moments where it singled  out the controller even poking fun at  how the sixaxis didn't have haptic  feedback  [Applause]  the PlayStation 4's Dualshock 4 was a  big step forward for the controller at  first glance it largely retained the  same distinctive shape and set of  features that previous controllers had  but it also introduced a bunch of new  upgrades that leaned further into online  gaming and entertainment effectively  doing away with the traditional start  and select buttons the new controller  added an options button as well as a  dedicated share button which allowed  gamers to share their favorite stills  and clips immediately on social media  the l2 and r2 buttons also saw an  upgrade which moved away from the flat  design from previous generations to a  more curved and trigger like shape  making them more comfortable to use  during action-oriented games with a new  light bar that made the top plate of the  controller glow in different colors the  Dual Shock 4 also added a touch pad on  the front center of the device which was  commonly used as a way to access menus  and other functions other add-ons  included a small speaker just below the  touchpad that would project in-game  sounds as well as a 3.5 millimeter audio  jack which allowed players to plug in  their preferred headsets in a later  update the DualShock 4 added a visible  light strip at the top of the touchpad  and the ability to use the controller as  a dedicated wired controller via USB  which wasn't possible with previous  models compared to other consoles the  playstation controller has been one of  the more visually consistent devices  giving it an iconic presence over the  last 26 years and while the dual sense  has most of the DualShock for  functionality it also boasts a handful  of new features like a USB type-c port  built-in mic a mysterious create button  in place of the share button and a  completely out of left field two-tone  design another feature that has the  gaming community intrigued is the  introduction of adaptive triggers an  advanced haptic feedback like pulling a  string on a bow or shooting a large gun  this new feature can potentially  replicate the feeling of tension and  force giving players a distinctive  real-time response during gameplay which  is your favorite PlayStation controller  and are you excited to try out the dual  sense let us know in the comment section  down below and stay tuned to game spot  for all ps5 coverage  [Music]  [Music]
 

